WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.840
Back from the dead? Tiny Elvis? There are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.440
TV and movie performances of&nbsp;
The King nobody could forget!

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.660
One of the most surreal intersections of&nbsp;
politics and pop culture happened on December 21,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:18.180
1970. Elvis Presley visited President Richard&nbsp;
Nixon at the White House in the hopes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.820
becoming deputized as a federal narcotics agent.&nbsp;
Presley, though no stranger to illegal substances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.260
was reportedly disturbed by the youth&nbsp;
drug culture of the day and wished to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.880
lend his support and influence to the war&nbsp;
on drugs. A photo was taken of the two men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.380
and Presley was presented with his very own&nbsp;
badge. There's no world where this encounter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.640
is anything but hilarious, or at least that's&nbsp;
what "Rock and Roll High School" director Allan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.340
Arkush must have thought when he helmed the&nbsp;
1997 Showtime mockumentary "Elvis Meets Nixon."

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:52.620
"Hosted" by Dick Cavett as himself, what's most&nbsp;
surprising about the film is how straight it plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:57.240
the facts of the meeting. Longtime television&nbsp;
actor Rick Peters doesn't much look like Elvis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.780
but embodies The King's swagger&nbsp;
and charisma better than most,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.660
even from underneath yet another terrible wig.

00:01:03.824 --> 00:01:05.084
"Well, thank you very much."

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.320
Apparently, the story of Presley and Nixon's&nbsp;
White House meeting was so nice they told it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.620
twice. Nearly 20 years later, another version of&nbsp;
the story was produced by Amazon Studios in 2016&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:23.040
as "Elvis &amp; Nixon," featuring Kevin Spacey as&nbsp;
Nixon and Michael Shannon as Presley. Director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.680
Liza Johnson keeps the pace up with a jazzy score&nbsp;
and heist movie energy, as Presley guilelessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.880
gets on a plane and arranges a meeting with&nbsp;
the White House. Whereas in Arkush's film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.320
the meeting is little more than a button at the&nbsp;
end of the story, here the meeting takes up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.540
final half hour of the film. Spacey's full-body&nbsp;
transformation into Nixon clashes with Shannon's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.900
minimalist-to-the-point-of-nonexistent&nbsp;
impersonation of Elvis.

00:01:46.269 --> 00:01:48.669
"I don't know if I even know who he is anymore."

00:01:48.669 --> 00:01:53.700
The film's placement on this list is tricky,&nbsp;
and not just because of the distaste audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.320
today might feel toward Spacey. Michael&nbsp;
Shannon is arguably the finest actor here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.540
but he is so miscast as Elvis that it feels&nbsp;
like an intentional choice. His gaunt face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.920
and Midwestern rasp bear no resemblance&nbsp;
to Presley. But at the same time, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.007
is something admirable in Shannon's commitment to&nbsp;
the character as a man first and an icon second.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:19.140
The 2005 Johnny Cash biopic "Walk the Line"&nbsp;
doesn't feature Elvis as a character very much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.880
Though he and Cash both got their starts&nbsp;
at Sun Records in Memphis and toured the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.200
Southern circuit together, their stories&nbsp;
diverge at a certain point. Until then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.640
though, Elvis serves as a potent symbol&nbsp;
in the James Mangold-directed film. Cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.900
first sees Elvis on the street in Memphis&nbsp;
and follows him to the Sun Records studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.720
until owner Sam Phillips slams the door shut in&nbsp;
Cash's face. Later, as Cash's career begins to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.280
take off and they're booked together on the same&nbsp;
tour, he tries amphetamines for the first time on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.680
Elvis' recommendation. And as Johnny cheats on his&nbsp;
wife backstage with a local woman, the sound of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:57.120
Presley singing "That's All Right Mama" creeps&nbsp;
up in the sound mix — specifically, the line:

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:04.860
Actor and singer-songwriter Tyler Hilton&nbsp;
plays Presley and performs his own songs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.600
a rarity for Elvises on film. Vocally&nbsp;
he doesn't sound very much like Presley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.440
but he has a good voice, and his natural&nbsp;
confidence and charisma as a musician&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.300
makes him a fine substitute. It makes sense&nbsp;
why the crowds in the film would treat him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.440
like he's Elvis, and why Phoenix's Cash&nbsp;
would respect and be intimidated by him.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.080
Rumors and beliefs that Elvis had faked his death&nbsp;
became widespread, either seriously or in jest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:34.920
almost immediately after his sudden death in 1977&nbsp;
at age 42. But despite this, there are hardly any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:40.140
films or television shows in the "Elvis Lives!"&nbsp;
genre. One of the few is David Winkler's 1998&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:45.780
road trip indie dramedy "Finding Graceland." The&nbsp;
film stars Jonathan Schaech as Byron, a grieving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.640
young widower who gives a ride to an older man&nbsp;
who claims to be Elvis, played by Harvey Keitel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.500
He's trying to make his way to Graceland in time&nbsp;
to attend a party in honor of his "death." Along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.600
the way, as often happens in films like this, the&nbsp;
two strangers share adventures and life lessons.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:05.400
Keitel seems wildly miscast at first,&nbsp;
looking or sounding nothing like Presley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.420
But there's a twinkle in his eye, and&nbsp;
a sense of fun in his performance that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.620
feels like a piece of Presley's own screen&nbsp;
persona. Whether or not he really is Elvis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.609
he's good company; why would you want to&nbsp;
spoil that by knowing one way or another?

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:23.520
The most notable "Elvis Lives!" film is the&nbsp;
2002 horror-comedy "Bubba Ho-Tep," directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.500
by Don Coscarelli. An aged Elvis, played by&nbsp;
Bruce Campbell, and John F. Kennedy do battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:33.180
with a resurrected Egyptian mummy terrorizing&nbsp;
the east Texas retirement home where they live.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:38.220
At least one of these men may not be who he&nbsp;
says he is, but the film mostly takes Elvis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:42.900
at his word that he is the real deal. Like&nbsp;
Rick Peters in "Elvis Meets Nixon," Campbell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:47.820
is freed by the film's comedic tone to play&nbsp;
Presley as a swaggering cartoon, which in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.466
way feels more realistic than some of the more&nbsp;
dramatic, low-key performances on this list.

00:04:52.466 --> 00:04:57.304
"Just what are you getting at, Elvis?" "I think&nbsp;
you know what I'm getting at, Mr. President.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.464
We're gonna kill us a mummy."

00:05:00.464 --> 00:05:04.980
The film itself is undercooked, padded out&nbsp;
to hit its already slim running time. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.520
is at times refreshingly forthright&nbsp;
about the indignities of old age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.028
but just as often playing old&nbsp;
people falling down for laughs.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:16.740
One of the most popular depictions of Elvis&nbsp;
in the last 20 years has not been on film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:20.220
but on stage. The mega-popular&nbsp;
jukebox musical "Million Dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:26.100
Quartet" tells the story of a day in 1956&nbsp;
when Presley, Johnny Cash, Jerry Lee Lewis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:31.860
and Carl Perkins all happened to be at Sun&nbsp;
Records at the same time. In 2017 writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:38.280
Colin Escot and Floyd Mutrux adapted and expanded&nbsp;
the show into the CMT miniseries "Sun Records."

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:43.740
Musician and Elvis impersonator Drake Milligan&nbsp;
plays Presley as a high schooler swept up into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:48.540
the burgeoning world of rock and roll. Thus far&nbsp;
the series is his only acting credit other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:53.760
a 2014 short film where he also played Elvis.&nbsp;
He's been a contestant on both "American Idol"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:58.200
and "America's Got Talent," and above all&nbsp;
he has the moves, the look, and the sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:02.820
of Presley down to a science. The series is a&nbsp;
handsome gloss on those rough-and-tumble years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:06.972
and asks little of Milligan other than&nbsp;
to be handsome and glossy in return.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.020
In all the decades of actors&nbsp;
playing Elvis on screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.100
it apparently never occurred to anyone&nbsp;
to cast an actual rock star in the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:20.160
until Jack White's one-scene cameo in the&nbsp;
vastly underrated 2007 parody "Walk Hard:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.620
The Dewey Cox Story." John C. Reilly plays&nbsp;
the eponymous rock music prodigy, Dewey.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:30.360
White appears in a scene taking place in&nbsp;
the 1950s, showing up just long enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:34.484
aggressively mumble a karate-based threat&nbsp;
to Dewey for the crime of talking to him.

00:06:34.484 --> 00:06:39.344
"There's two things you need to know. I'm&nbsp;
the king, and number two is look out, man!"

00:06:39.344 --> 00:06:43.200
Perhaps before White there was never a rock&nbsp;
star with the improbable combination of having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:48.420
a passable Elvis impersonation and being a big&nbsp;
enough fan to lovingly skewer The King's persona.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:53.400
He's Elvis. He's very small. He spends&nbsp;
his days marveling at how much bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.840
everyone and everything is than he. That's&nbsp;
the premise and single joke of "Tiny Elvis,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.940
a 1992 "Saturday Night Live" sketch from&nbsp;
Nicolas Cage's first and only time as host.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:07.680
Cage has long been an avowed Elvis fan, and&nbsp;
many of his early roles, from "Rumble Fish"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.700
to "Raising Arizona" to "Wild at Heart,"&nbsp;
are played at a certain rockabilly pitch.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:16.560
The sketch has two scenes that follow the same&nbsp;
pattern: Elvis sits around with Memphis Mafia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.940
members Sonny and Red West delighting in&nbsp;
how huge everything is. But when one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:25.664
them takes it too far and calls him "cute"&nbsp;
due to his size, Elvis flies into a rage.

00:07:25.664 --> 00:07:27.574
"Well that's it! Out of the car, all ya!"

00:07:27.574 --> 00:07:30.660
On one hand, the sketch is little&nbsp;
more than a vehicle for Cage's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:34.440
above-average Elvis impersonation&nbsp;
and some impressive-for-live-TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:38.160
blue screen work. But in pairing&nbsp;
Tiny E with his entourage members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.789
it lands a satirical shot at the way Elvis&nbsp;
was coddled and enabled by those around him.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:49.920
Irish actor Jonathan Rhys-Myers donned the sequins&nbsp;
and sideburns in the CBS 2005 miniseries "Elvis,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:55.560
which covers The King's early days in Memphis up&nbsp;
through his 1968 "Comeback Special." That concert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.720
works here as a natural climax that allows Presley&nbsp;
to go out on top in a way he didn't in real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.160
and also spares the whisper-thin Rhys-Myers&nbsp;
the indignity of wearing a fat suit.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:09.180
Rhys-Myers makes for an intense,&nbsp;
live-wire Presley in his private moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:13.260
though a less-than-convincing singer&nbsp;
on stage. The miniseries makes the odd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.440
choice to have him lip sync to the&nbsp;
studio recordings of Elvis' songs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:20.160
even when he's meant to be performing&nbsp;
live. The series and Rhys-Myers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.600
refreshingly unafraid to show Presley as&nbsp;
unsympathetic and even something of a weirdo.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.820
The short-lived HBO rock and roll series&nbsp;
"Vinyl" was perhaps, above all, a failure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:36.180
of imagination. Set in 1971 New York City, it&nbsp;
follows bad boy record executive Richie Finestra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:40.620
as he hustles everyone in his life on behalf&nbsp;
of his beloved label, the fictional American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:45.480
Century. Considering that it was executive&nbsp;
produced by Mick Jagger and Martin Scorsese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.620
it's all the more disappointing how often&nbsp;
the series falls back on tired clichés.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:53.820
One episode, however, breaks the mold.&nbsp;
In "The King and I," Richie and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:58.080
second-in-command hear a rumor that Elvis is&nbsp;
considering leaving his longtime record label,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:03.120
RCA-Victor. They take a plane to Las Vegas to&nbsp;
have a meeting with Presley in an effort to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.500
him to sign with American Century. In addition&nbsp;
to bearing a striking physical resemblance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:12.300
professional impersonator Shawn Klush gives&nbsp;
a surprisingly tender one-scene performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:16.920
He sheds light on The King in an era that is&nbsp;
too-often played for laughs or drug-fueled drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:21.129
trapped in a gilded cage above the&nbsp;
Vegas strip, unable to break free.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.820
In 2019 it was announced that Austin Butler, a&nbsp;
former Nickelodeon and Disney Channel heartthrob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:30.600
recently seen getting mauled to death by&nbsp;
Brad Pitt's dog in "Once Upon a Time...In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:35.700
Hollywood," was cast as Elvis. Nearly three&nbsp;
years later, director Baz Luhrmann's dizzying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:40.800
gaudy biopic of The King premiered to rapturous&nbsp;
applause at the 2022 Cannes Film Festival.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.300
Butler, more than nearly every other actor on&nbsp;
this list, put in the work to become Elvis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:49.800
He does his own singing, dancing, and&nbsp;
guitar playing in the film. In return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:53.280
Luhrmann's camera worships him from&nbsp;
head to toe, especially in the parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:58.500
of the film detailing Presley's 1950s heyday&nbsp;
when he was young, beautiful, and dangerous.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:02.880
The screenplay covers The King's entire&nbsp;
life and beyond, perhaps biting off more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.720
than it can chew. As good as Butler is and&nbsp;
as much effort as he is clearly giving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:11.700
Presley's hedonistic final years don't fit him&nbsp;
quite as well as the pink-suited mama's boy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:16.140
his early years. And like many actors before&nbsp;
him, playing Elvis seems to have left a mark;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:20.279
it's been months since filming completed,&nbsp;
but you can still hear it in his voice.

00:10:20.279 --> 00:10:25.359
"And it's hard to, in the beginning,&nbsp;
feel anything but small compared to him."

00:10:25.359 --> 00:10:30.660
The best Elvis was the first Elvis. In&nbsp;
1979, just two years after Presley's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:35.700
ABC aired the miniseries "Elvis." It covers&nbsp;
Presley's early days through his Army stint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:40.080
his movie career, and up through his comeback&nbsp;
special and the triumphant early Vegas days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:45.240
What sets the 1979 miniseries apart from&nbsp;
its decades-later counterpart or any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:50.400
the other films on this list are two things: The&nbsp;
patient, painterly filmmaking of John Carpenter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.580
and the megawatt charisma of&nbsp;
Kurt Russell in the title role.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:58.080
What's most remarkable about Russell's performance&nbsp;
here is the way that he manages to make a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:03.000
character out of Presley while also working in&nbsp;
all of the famous "Elvis impersonation" tics:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.960
the voice, the pout, the swivel.&nbsp;
Even his sideburns look good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:10.000
which would have been enough alone to&nbsp;
put him at the top of the rankings.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:11.560
"Man, I been away too long."

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:15.420
Perhaps he picked up some mojo when he had&nbsp;
appeared as a child actor in Presley's "It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:19.200
Happened at the World's Fair." 15 years&nbsp;
later Russell would return to the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:23.040
providing the voice to Peter Dobson's&nbsp;
body in "Forrest Gump," and would wink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:28.020
at it in 2001's other Vegas-set heist&nbsp;
movie, "3000 Miles to Graceland."

